INSERT INTO `blog_posts` VALUES (1,'Sliding vs. Hinged Wardrobe Doors: Which Works Better for Your Bedroom?','sliding-vs-hinged-wardrobe-doors','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','The sliding vs. hinged wardrobe doors decision is really an access decision. The door has to work with your bed, the room’s walking route, the wardrobe interior, and the way you get dressed. A front that looks neat in a showroom can be frustrating when its panels hide the drawer you need or its doors leave no comfortable place to stand.','The sliding vs. hinged wardrobe doors decision is really an access decision. The door has to work with your bed, the room’s walking route, the wardrobe interior, and the way you get dressed. A front that looks neat in a showroom can be frustrating when its panels hide the drawer you need or its doors leave no comfortable place to stand.\n\nSliding doors are useful when an outward swing would obstruct the room. Hinged doors can provide direct access to individual compartments when their swing has enough space. Neither system is automatically better quality. The right choice depends on the complete wardrobe, its hardware, and the bedroom around it.\n\nA hinged door rotates into the room on a swing arc. Its operating space depends on the door-leaf width, handle, hinge arrangement, and opening angle. Narrower leaves may reduce the projection, but that can change the number of doors and the internal compartment layout.\n\nA sliding door moves along a track, usually overlapping another panel. It avoids the same outward swing but occupies depth within the front assembly and leaves only part of the wardrobe open at a time, depending on the system.\n\nConsider the person using the wardrobe as part of its footprint. Someone must stand in front of the opening, bend toward a lower drawer, reach a hanger, and step back with clothing. A door that moves successfully is only one part of a usable design.\n\nDo not use the table to assume one system is always cheaper, quieter, or longer-lasting. Those outcomes depend on the specified components, installation, use, and available repair support.\n\nMeasure from the finished wardrobe front to the nearest bed, chair, or wall. Then mark a hinged door’s actual swing on the floor. Check the arrangement with a person standing where they would normally dress.\n\nFor an illustrative example, a 450 mm-wide door opening into a 600 mm gap can leave only about 150 mm between the outer edge of the open leaf and the opposing furniture at the tightest section. The door may technically open, but that does not establish comfortable access. A user might need to stand to one side, and an internal drawer could make the situation worse.','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','5 min read','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22'),(2,'Small Bedroom Layouts for Dhaka Flats: Fitting a Bed, Wardrobe, and Clear Walkways','small-bedroom-layouts-dhaka-flats','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','The challenge with small bedroom layouts for Dhaka flats is not simply fitting a bed and wardrobe inside four walls. You also need to get out of bed, open storage, reach the window, and walk to the bathroom or bedroom door without turning sideways around furniture.','The challenge with small bedroom layouts for Dhaka flats is not simply fitting a bed and wardrobe inside four walls. You also need to get out of bed, open storage, reach the window, and walk to the bathroom or bedroom door without turning sideways around furniture.\n\nA room can look spacious in a rendering with every drawer closed, yet feel cramped during ordinary use. The most useful layout begins with the largest furniture footprints and the movements around them. Once those work, lighting, finishes, and decoration become much easier to choose.\n\nMeasure the room after allowing for plaster, skirting, built-out columns, and other fixed projections. Record door swings, window openings, curtain positions, electrical outlets, the air conditioner, and any access panel.\n\nMark the bedroom entrance, bathroom route, and balcony access. These routes should remain usable when a wardrobe door or bedside drawer is open. Do not assume a bathroom door can be moved without checking the surrounding services and construction.\n\nThen measure the bed itself. Mattress size is not the same as the bed-frame footprint: side rails, upholstery, a projecting footboard, and a deep headboard can add to both length and width. Ask for the manufacturer’s overall dimensions or a measured shop drawing.\n\nUse the same discipline for the wardrobe. Record finished depth including doors, handles, and any sliding track. Internal hanging space and external projection are different measurements. A cabinet can look deep on a drawing but lose useful interior space to its construction and door system.\n\nCentering a shared bed on a suitable wall can give both sleepers independent side access. A wardrobe opposite the foot may use the remaining wall efficiently, but it places storage operation in the same space as the foot-of-bed walkway.\n\nMeasure that overlap carefully. A hinged wardrobe door or extended drawer can occupy much of the gap. Use the actual door width and drawer extension before choosing this arrangement.','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','8 min read','2026-10-08 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22'),(3,'L-Shaped vs. U-Shaped vs. Parallel Kitchens: Choosing the Right Layout','l-shaped-u-shaped-parallel-kitchen-layouts','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','Comparing L-shaped vs. U-shaped vs. parallel kitchens starts with the room, not with which layout looks most impressive. The right arrangement gives you useful preparation space, accessible storage, and enough room to open appliances without interrupting every other activity. The wrong arrangement can fill the kitchen with cupboards while leaving very little usable workspace.','Comparing L-shaped vs. U-shaped vs. parallel kitchens starts with the room, not with which layout looks most impressive. The right arrangement gives you useful preparation space, accessible storage, and enough room to open appliances without interrupting every other activity. The wrong arrangement can fill the kitchen with cupboards while leaving very little usable workspace.\n\nFor an apartment kitchen in Bangladesh, record the doors, windows, plumbing, cooking point, and exhaust possibilities before choosing a shape. Then compare how each option supports the sequence of taking out ingredients, washing, preparing, cooking, and serving. Cabinet quantity should follow that workflow, not replace it.\n\nBegin with finished internal dimensions. Measure at more than one position where walls are uneven, and record projecting columns, skirting, window sills, and service boxes. A nominal room width does not necessarily equal the usable width between installed cabinet fronts.\n\nMark the door position and swing, the refrigerator’s full opening, and the location of any balcony or utility access. Identify what can reasonably move and what requires professional review. A sink relocation may involve drainage constraints; a stove relocation may affect gas or electrical services and the exhaust route.\n\nList the exact appliances before finalizing cabinet openings. A refrigerator needs more than its body width to operate as intended, and a dishwasher or oven needs space with its door open. Use the actual manufacturer’s installation and operating dimensions.\n\nFinally, decide whether the kitchen is a destination or a passage. A room that connects two busy areas must handle through-traffic differently from one entered through a single doorway.\n\nA single-wall layout is also worth considering when the room is too narrow for facing counters. Do not force one of the three named options into a room that cannot support it. Likewise, an island or peninsula should be added only after the basic circulation works.\n\nAn L-shaped kitchen places cabinets along two adjoining walls. Its open side can help connect the kitchen to a dining area or preserve movement through the room. It may also make it easier to position tall storage away from the central preparation area.','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','6 min read','2026-10-03 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22'),(4,'Open Kitchen vs. Closed Kitchen in Bangladesh: Which Should You Choose?','open-vs-closed-kitchen-bangladesh','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','Choosing an open kitchen vs. closed kitchen in Bangladesh is less about following a style and more about understanding daily cooking. A kitchen that looks inviting during a viewing may feel very different while someone is frying, washing large pots, entertaining visitors, and preparing the next meal at the same time.','Choosing an open kitchen vs. closed kitchen in Bangladesh is less about following a style and more about understanding daily cooking. A kitchen that looks inviting during a viewing may feel very different while someone is frying, washing large pots, entertaining visitors, and preparing the next meal at the same time.\n\nAn open kitchen favors connection with the living or dining area. A closed kitchen provides a clearer physical boundary. A semi-open kitchen offers some control over that connection. None of these formats is automatically best, and none removes the need for suitable ventilation, safe services, and a workable cabinet layout.\n\nAn open kitchen has a substantial direct connection to the adjoining living or dining space, without a closable wall across that connection. A peninsula or counter may define the boundary, but it does not enclose the cooking area.\n\nA closed kitchen is a separate room with walls and a door. Its performance depends on the door, openings, ventilation, and construction, not simply the fact that it has walls.\n\nA semi-open kitchen falls between those arrangements. It may use sliding glass panels, a serving hatch, or a partially open boundary. Describe the actual design rather than relying on the label. A kitchen with a large permanent opening behaves differently from one that can close with a full-width glazed partition.\n\nAlso separate enclosure from layout. An L-shaped, U-shaped, or parallel kitchen can be open or closed. First decide how the kitchen should relate to the home, then select the cabinet arrangement that fits its dimensions.\n\nUse this as a discussion tool, not a scorecard. A household that values preparation privacy may willingly accept a more enclosed room. Another may prefer openness and design its storage and cleaning routine around that choice.\n\nThink through a typical week rather than a special occasion. Does your household prepare several cooked meals a day? Is frying common? Do two people work in the kitchen together? Are dishes washed continuously or left until after guests leave?','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','4 min read','2026-09-28 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22'),(5,'TV Wall Design Ideas: Storage, Cable Management, and Viewing Comfort','tv-wall-design-storage-cable-management','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','The best TV wall design ideas begin with the people watching the screen, not the decorative panel behind it. A successful television wall puts the picture in a comfortable position, stores the equipment you actually own, hides wiring safely, and remains easy to service. A large feature wall can do all of that, but a simple console and painted wall can also work beautifully.','The best TV wall design ideas begin with the people watching the screen, not the decorative panel behind it. A successful television wall puts the picture in a comfortable position, stores the equipment you actually own, hides wiring safely, and remains easy to service. A large feature wall can do all of that, but a simple console and painted wall can also work beautifully.\n\nFor an apartment living room, treat the television, furniture, wiring, lighting, and seating as one plan. Choosing each separately often leaves the screen too high, the console too deep, or the sockets trapped behind an inaccessible panel.\n\nMake an equipment inventory before drawing the cabinet. Include the TV, set-top box, streaming device, soundbar, game console, router if needed, and any other device that will live nearby. Write down each item’s dimensions, connection points, and manufacturer-required ventilation clearance.\n\nSeparate equipment storage from ordinary storage. Remotes and spare cables can go in a drawer. A device that produces heat or needs line-of-sight remote control may require a different arrangement. Add space for the plug, cable bend, and your hand to reach the connection, not just the equipment body.\n\nMark the main viewing seat and any secondary seats. Then assess candidate TV walls from those positions. Consider window reflections, the route to other rooms, and whether the screen will be viewed mostly straight on or from an angle.\n\nDo not center the TV on the entire wall if that places it noticeably off-center from the seats used every day. Visual balance can come from the surrounding cabinet composition rather than forcing the screen into a geometrically centered but uncomfortable location.\n\nScreen diagonal and viewing distance should be considered together. Samsung publishes a starting reference of approximately 1.2 times the screen diagonal for an immersive viewing position. It is a manufacturer guideline, not a medical minimum or a requirement for every viewer.⁠\n\nThe following calculations use that reference and round to the nearest centimeter:','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','5 min read','2026-09-23 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22'),(6,'Small Living Room Design for Dhaka Apartments: Layout and Storage Ideas','small-living-room-design-dhaka-apartments','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','Good small living room design for Dhaka apartments starts with a simple question: what must the room let your family do comfortably? A room may need to welcome visitors, provide a place to watch television, hold school bags, and connect the entrance to the dining area or balcony. Buying the largest sofa that fits between two walls does not resolve those competing needs.','Good small living room design for Dhaka apartments starts with a simple question: what must the room let your family do comfortably? A room may need to welcome visitors, provide a place to watch television, hold school bags, and connect the entrance to the dining area or balcony. Buying the largest sofa that fits between two walls does not resolve those competing needs.\n\nThe better approach is to protect movement first, choose seating for everyday use, and create storage for belongings that otherwise collect on visible surfaces. Decoration comes after those decisions. You can achieve a comfortable result with ready-made furniture, fitted joinery, or a carefully chosen combination.\n\nDraw the finished room from above. Record wall lengths, door openings, windows, columns, projecting corners, electrical outlets, and the position of the air conditioner. Measure the usable floor area rather than relying only on the dimensions in a sales brochure.\n\nMark the routes people follow: entrance to bedroom, sofa to dining table, and living room to balcony. Include the full door swing and the space needed to open curtains. A furniture footprint includes its arms, projecting handles, reclined position, and any part that extends during use.\n\nDo not treat a route as spare storage space simply because nobody is standing there in a photograph. If the balcony is used for everyday tasks, its access should remain practical when everyone is seated.\n\nList the room’s regular users and activities. A household that mainly watches television needs a different arrangement from one that hosts long conversations. A family sharing the room with a work corner may value a compact desk more than a second display cabinet.\n\nSeparate everyday seating from visitor seating. Four comfortable permanent seats plus two movable stools may suit the room better than a heavy matching sofa set. Store school bags, chargers, newspapers, and remotes near the places where people actually use them.\n\nA straight sofa with one compact accent chair creates an adaptable starting point. Place the chair where it supports conversation without cutting across the main route. A lightweight second chair can move closer when visitors arrive.','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','6 min read','2026-09-18 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22'),(7,'Living and Dining Room Partition Ideas for Bangladeshi Apartments','living-dining-room-partition-ideas-bangladesh','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','The most useful living and dining room partition ideas solve a specific problem without creating a new one. You may want to screen the dining table from the main entrance, add crockery storage, or make the living area feel more settled. A divider that achieves that but blocks daylight or traps dining chairs is not a good trade.','The most useful living and dining room partition ideas solve a specific problem without creating a new one. You may want to screen the dining table from the main entrance, add crockery storage, or make the living area feel more settled. A divider that achieves that but blocks daylight or traps dining chairs is not a good trade.\n\nIn a Bangladeshi apartment, start with the complete shared room rather than choosing a partition from a catalog. The position of windows, family circulation, and the way visitors use the space should decide how much separation you need. Sometimes a low cabinet is enough. Sometimes a closable screen is worthwhile. Sometimes no physical partition is the better answer.\n\nWrite one main purpose for the divider. Is it to interrupt the entrance sightline, display objects, hold serving dishes, or create a private zone for occasional use?\n\nThese goals need different solutions. Open shelves can organize a boundary while remaining visually connected. A half-height cabinet can hold crockery and provide a serving surface. A full-height sliding partition can close an opening, but its track, parked panels, and maintenance requirements occupy real space.\n\nAvoid asking one narrow divider to perform every job. Combining a TV, display niches, storage doors, and a circulation opening can produce a bulky structure that overwhelms a modest room.\n\nVertical slats can block a direct view from one position while remaining open from another. Reeded glass obscures detail but may still reveal silhouettes, especially when the other side is brightly lit.\n\nNeither automatically provides acoustic privacy. Open shelving, curtains, and slatted screens leave paths for sound. A room that needs meaningful speech privacy requires a properly specified enclosure, including its doors, seals, and adjoining construction. Do not pay for a decorative divider on the assumption that it will behave like a sound-isolating wall.\n\nIdentify which windows light each zone. If the living area contains the only substantial window, the dining area may depend on borrowed daylight through the shared space.','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','5 min read','2026-09-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22'),(8,'Living Room Interior Design in Bangladesh: Designing the Space That Represents Your Family','living-room-interior-design-in-bangladesh','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','In Bangladesh, the living room carries a weight that few other spaces in the home can match. It is the first room a guest sees, the room where family gathers after iftar, and the room that quietly communicates your family’s values without a single word being spoken. Getting living room interior design in Bangladesh right means understanding not just proportion and palette, but the specific cultural rhythms, climate pressures, and spatial realities that shape how families actually live in Dhaka.','In Bangladesh, the living room carries a weight that few other spaces in the home can match. It is the first room a guest sees, the room where family gathers after iftar, and the room that quietly communicates your family’s values without a single word being spoken. Getting living room interior design in Bangladesh right means understanding not just proportion and palette, but the specific cultural rhythms, climate pressures, and spatial realities that shape how families actually live in Dhaka.\n\nIn most Western design contexts, the living room is primarily a family retreat. In Bangladesh, it functions simultaneously as a family space and a formal reception room, and these two roles place competing demands on every design decision. Guests arrive frequently and with little advance notice. Relatives, colleagues, neighbors, and community elders all expect to be received properly, which means seating must be abundant, sight lines must feel generous, and the overall impression must project hospitality without appearing ostentatious.\n\nThis dual obligation is one of the most challenging problems in Bangladeshi residential design. Families in Dhaka apartments typically have between 120 and 200 square feet dedicated to the living area, and within that footprint they must accommodate a formal sofa arrangement for guests, a comfortable viewing angle toward the television for family evenings, and often a transition zone that blurs into the dining area. The solution is rarely a single piece of furniture or a clever trick. It requires a considered spatial strategy developed from the first draft of the floor plan.\n\nWe at Triangle Interior Design have worked through this challenge across more than 500 projects spanning Gulshan, Bashundhara, Mirpur, and Uttara, and in every case the starting point is the same: understanding exactly who uses this room, how often, and for what purpose before a single sofa is specified.\n\nDhaka apartments were built across several decades of varying planning philosophy. Older buildings in Dhanmondi and Wari tend to have clearly demarcated rooms with solid walls separating the living area from the dining and kitchen zones. Newer residential towers in Bashundhara and Purbachal often feature more open-plan arrangements, sometimes for genuine design reasons and sometimes simply because the builder needed to make a smaller footprint feel larger on a brochure.\n\nBoth layouts have genuine merit and genuine drawbacks for the Bangladeshi household, and the right choice depends on family composition, guest volume, and lifestyle rather than on trends.\n\nFor families with frequent formal guests, a closed or semi-closed living room generally performs better in the Bangladeshi context. The ability to control what guests see and smell, combined with the cooling efficiency advantage during the April-to-October heat season, makes defined boundaries a practical rather than merely aesthetic choice. For smaller nuclear families with fewer formal hosting obligations, an open plan that borrows visual space from the dining area can make a compact apartment feel genuinely airy.\n\nThe sofa arrangement in a Bangladeshi living room is not simply a furniture placement decision. It is a social architecture decision. In a culture where guests are typically seated facing the hosts, and where the hierarchy of seating carries real social meaning, the configuration of sofas determines how conversations flow and how comfortable guests feel.','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','5 min read','2026-09-08 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22'),(9,'What Affects Interior Design Cost in Bangladesh?','what-affects-interior-design-cost-bangladesh','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','What affects interior design cost in Bangladesh? The biggest factors are the work scope, condition of the property, number and type of rooms, quantity of built-in furniture, material and hardware specifications, electrical or plumbing changes, site access, project timing, taxes, and changes made after approval.','What affects interior design cost in Bangladesh? The biggest factors are the work scope, condition of the property, number and type of rooms, quantity of built-in furniture, material and hardware specifications, electrical or plumbing changes, site access, project timing, taxes, and changes made after approval.\n\nFloor area matters, but it does not explain the whole budget. Two equal-size flats can cost very different amounts when one needs extensive cabinetry and technical work while the other needs selected rooms. A useful estimate begins with drawings, quantities, and specifications, not one price per square foot.\n\nThis guide explains the budgeting logic behind a proposal. For the complete planning sequence, use the planned interior design guide for Bangladesh.\n\nScope is the first cost driver because it defines what the project team must design, produce, install, and supervise. “Full home interior” is too broad for accurate budgeting. A proper scope should name each room and each component within it.\n\nFor example, a living room may include a TV unit, sofa, display storage, wall finish, lighting, ceiling work, curtains, and decor. Another living room may include only layout planning and loose furniture. Both are living room projects, but the quantities and trades are not comparable.\n\nAsk for a room-by-room scope before discussing the total. If needed, classify items as essential for move-in, useful later, or decorative upgrades.\n\nThe starting condition changes the work. A newly handed-over flat may still need surface correction, service coordination, or changes to developer-provided fittings. An occupied home can require protection, dismantling, debris removal, temporary storage, and phased access. An older property may reveal damaged surfaces or outdated services only after work begins.\n\nDo not assume every alteration is cosmetic. Moving walls, changing wet areas, modifying electrical loads, or affecting fire and life-safety systems needs proper technical review. Bangladesh publishes theBangladesh National Building Code 2020, while RAJUK lists the applicableDhaka building laws and regulations. Your designer should identify when building management approval or specialist input is needed.','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','7 min read','2026-09-03 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22'),(10,'Interior Design BOQ Guide for Bangladesh: How to Read a Quotation Line by Line','interior-design-boq-guide-bangladesh','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','An interior design BOQ in Bangladesh should tell you what work is included, how it is measured, how much is allowed, the rate for each unit, and the resulting amount. Read it in this order: document details, scope sections, item descriptions, units, quantities, unit rates, totals, exclusions, taxes, payment terms, and variation rules.','An interior design BOQ in Bangladesh should tell you what work is included, how it is measured, how much is allowed, the rate for each unit, and the resulting amount. Read it in this order: document details, scope sections, item descriptions, units, quantities, unit rates, totals, exclusions, taxes, payment terms, and variation rules.\n\nDo not begin with the grand total. A low total can hide missing hardware, vague materials, unpriced work, or provisional quantities. A higher total may include design, production, delivery, installation, supervision, and taxes. The BOQ reveals the difference.\n\nThis homeowner guide explains each column and contract term without turning your home into a public tender. For the broader process, read the planned interior design guide for Bangladesh.\n\nBOQ means Bill of Quantities, a structured schedule used to describe and price work. Bangladesh Public Procurement Authority guidance says it should provide enough quantity information for accurate pricing and a basis for valuing completed work. Its sample includes an item number, description, unit, quantity, unit rate, and amount.\n\nA home interior BOQ can be simpler than a government works document. Still, every meaningful amount should connect to a room, component, quantity, and specification. Drawings and commercial terms complete the picture.\n\nTheBPPA standard works documentuses this pattern and notes that quantities may be provisional in an admeasurement contract. Your agreement should state whether payment is fixed against an approved scope or adjusted to verified quantities.\n\nFirst confirm that the BOQ belongs to your project. The header should identify the client, property, date, version, company, and drawings used for measurement.\n\nLook for a validity period. On a revision, the version and date should make the current document obvious. Do not approve a total copied from an older design.','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','5 min read','2026-08-29 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22'),(11,'Interior Design Package Price in Bangladesh: What Should Be Included?','interior-design-package-price-bangladesh','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','An interior design package price in Bangladesh is useful only when the package clearly states what you will receive. Before comparing totals, check the rooms covered, design drawings, material specifications, furniture dimensions, execution work, supervision, delivery, installation, exclusions, payment stages, change rules, and handover documents. Two packages with the same name can contain very different scopes.','An interior design package price in Bangladesh is useful only when the package clearly states what you will receive. Before comparing totals, check the rooms covered, design drawings, material specifications, furniture dimensions, execution work, supervision, delivery, installation, exclusions, payment stages, change rules, and handover documents. Two packages with the same name can contain very different scopes.\n\nThere is no single standard package that suits every Bangladeshi home. A design-only service, a furniture package, and a complete turnkey service solve different problems. The safest choice is the package whose written scope matches your flat, lifestyle, existing site condition, and level of support.\n\nA clear home interior package should answer these questions before you approve it:\n\nA package price is a commercial offer for a defined group of services and products. It may cover only professional design, only selected furniture, or the coordinated delivery of a finished interior. The word “package” does not prove that everything is included.\n\nThe price depends on scope. Apartment size matters, but it is only one input. Room count, storage needs, existing civil condition, custom furniture, material choices, hardware, lighting, kitchen and bathroom work, access restrictions, and the amount of site coordination can all change what must be delivered. Our detailed guide towhat affects interior design cost in Bangladeshexplains these variables further.\n\nPackage names vary between companies. Use the following models as a comparison framework, not as universal definitions.\n\nA turnkey offer can reduce the number of parties you manage, but “turnkey” still needs a defined boundary. Read our guide to choosing aturnkey interior design company in Bangladeshbefore treating the label as proof of completeness.\n\nThe package should list every included space, such as the foyer, living room, dining area, bedrooms, kitchen, bathrooms, balcony, family space, or prayer corner. It should then list the work within each room.','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','7 min read','2026-08-24 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22'),(12,'Low-Budget Interior Design in Bangladesh: Where to Save and Where Not To','low-budget-interior-design-bangladesh-tips','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','The best low-budget interior design strategy in Bangladesh is to protect safety, moisture control, essential storage, ventilation, and frequently used hardware first. Save on decorative layers, complex shapes, excessive ceiling work, too many finishes, and furniture you can add later. A smaller, well-planned scope usually gives better value than filling every wall at once.','The best low-budget interior design strategy in Bangladesh is to protect safety, moisture control, essential storage, ventilation, and frequently used hardware first. Save on decorative layers, complex shapes, excessive ceiling work, too many finishes, and furniture you can add later. A smaller, well-planned scope usually gives better value than filling every wall at once.\n\nBegin with how your household lives. Prioritise the kitchen, bedrooms, essential lighting and electrical points, storage for daily items, and any bathroom or damp-area repairs. Keep the first phase simple, durable, and easy to maintain. Add feature walls, display units, loose decor, and upgraded finishes after the home works properly.\n\nThese low budget interior design Bangladesh tips focus on decisions, not artificial price promises. Your actual cost depends on measurements, existing conditions, material specifications, custom work, and the service scope you approve.\n\nUse this order for every room:\n\nThis sequence helps prevent a beautiful feature from consuming funds needed for a leaking pipe, an overloaded electrical layout, kitchen extraction, or a usable wardrobe.\n\nBangladesh’sNational Building Code 2020covers minimum standards for materials, construction, building services, safety, and alterations. When interior work affects those areas, use appropriately qualified professionals and follow building-management requirements.\n\nA minimum viable interior is a deliberate first phase. List what must be ready at move-in, what can wait, and what is purely aspirational.\n\nPhasing works best when the first design anticipates later additions. Plan future sockets, clearances, fixing points, appliance positions, and furniture dimensions now, even if you do not build every item now.','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','5 min read','2026-08-19 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22'),(13,'Home Renovation Before Interior Design in Bangladesh: What to Fix First','home-renovation-before-interior-design-bangladesh','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','Before choosing cabinets, wall panels, ceilings, or paint, assess the parts of your home that can damage those finishes later. For a home renovation and interior design project in Bangladesh, the first priorities are structural condition, moisture sources, electrical safety, plumbing, drainage, and the soundness of existing floors, walls, doors, and windows.','Before choosing cabinets, wall panels, ceilings, or paint, assess the parts of your home that can damage those finishes later. For a home renovation and interior design project in Bangladesh, the first priorities are structural condition, moisture sources, electrical safety, plumbing, drainage, and the soundness of existing floors, walls, doors, and windows.\n\nThe principle is simple: fix causes before covering symptoms. A beautiful finish cannot correct a leaking pipe, an unsafe circuit, a moving crack, or a damp wall. Early assessment gives your designer a reliable base, makes the quotation clearer, and reduces the chance of opening completed work again.\n\nStart with anything that affects safety, water control, or the stability of the proposed work. Then resolve hidden services and damaged substrates. Decorative layers should come last.\n\nThe Bangladesh National Building Code 2020 establishes minimum standards for the design, construction, use, and maintenance of buildings. Its provisions for existing buildings also address alteration, addition, and change of use. If a renovation affects structure, fire safety, building services, or an approved plan, seek competent advice instead of treating it as ordinary decoration.\n\nA homeowner should not decide whether a wall is structural by appearance. Columns, beams, slabs, shear walls, and service zones are part of a larger system. Even a masonry partition may conceal electrical or plumbing routes.\n\nAsk for professional assessment when you see significant cracking, exposed or corroded reinforcement, noticeable deflection, previous wall removal, slab cutting, or an alteration that does not match available drawings. The same rule applies when a new layout proposes a wider opening, a heavy built-in feature, or changes near structural members.\n\nYour interior designer can coordinate the layout, but structural judgment belongs with an appropriately qualified professional. Keep the approved recommendation with the project documents so the site team knows exactly what can remain, what needs repair, and what must not be touched.\n\nDo not hide an unexplained crack behind panelling. Record its location, seek the right assessment, and resolve the underlying concern before the decorative specification is finalised.','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','7 min read','2026-08-14 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22'),(14,'Interior Design Timeline in Bangladesh: How Long a Home Project Takes','interior-design-timeline-bangladesh','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','There is no universal interior design timeline for every home in Bangladesh. Duration depends on the approved scope, property condition, design decisions, custom production, material availability, site access, building rules, and how quickly required approvals are completed. A room refresh and a complete apartment cannot share one reliable schedule.','There is no universal interior design timeline for every home in Bangladesh. Duration depends on the approved scope, property condition, design decisions, custom production, material availability, site access, building rules, and how quickly required approvals are completed. A room refresh and a complete apartment cannot share one reliable schedule.\n\nTriangle Interior Design currently uses 45 to 90 days as a planning range for a typical home project. This range must be confirmed for your specific scope and is not a delivery guarantee. The useful question is not only, “How many days?” It is, “Which decisions and dependencies control those days?”\n\nA well-managed project moves through a connected sequence: brief, survey, concept, approval, technical planning, quotation, procurement, site preparation, production, installation, inspection, and handover. Some work can overlap after the design is sufficiently resolved, but premature overlap creates rework.\n\nThe RIBA Plan of Work progresses from briefing and concept through coordination, technical design, construction, handover, and use. Its transferable lesson is that each stage should enable the next.\n\nThe timeline begins when the project becomes clear, not when workers enter the site. A useful brief identifies the rooms, routines, storage, appliances, style, budget context, move-in window, and existing work.\n\nShare plans, handover papers, building rules, inspiration, and items you will keep. Identify who gives final household approval. Conflicting decisions can stall a strong schedule.\n\nAt Triangle Interior Design, thecomplete home interior design processbegins with understanding the people who will live in the home. That early clarity helps the team test priorities before time is spent detailing the wrong direction.\n\nMeasurements control furniture, cabinetry, ceiling levels, services, and quantities. Record columns, beams, openings, sill heights, floor changes, fixed services, and access constraints.','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','8 min read','2026-08-09 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22'),(15,'Interior Materials in Bangladesh: Boards, Laminates, Hardware, and Finishes','interior-materials-bangladesh-guide','Triangle Interior Design Editorial Team','Interior Design Insights','The best interior materials in Bangladesh are not one universal board, finish, or hardware range. The right specification depends on where the item will be used, its exposure to moisture and heat, the load it must carry, the finish you want, how often it will be handled, and how easily it can be maintained or repaired.','The best interior materials in Bangladesh are not one universal board, finish, or hardware range. The right specification depends on where the item will be used, its exposure to moisture and heat, the load it must carry, the finish you want, how often it will be handled, and how easily it can be maintained or repaired.\n\nA kitchen cabinet, bedroom wardrobe, bathroom vanity, wall panel, and dining table do different jobs. Giving all five the same vague description, such as “premium board,” hides the decisions that affect performance. A dependable proposal names the substrate, thickness where relevant, surface finish, edge treatment, hardware, fixing method, and approved sample for each item.\n\nUse this with the interior design guide for Bangladesh to make clear, room-specific choices.\n\nMaterial selection should begin with a performance brief. Record what the component must hold, how it will be used and cleaned, and whether it sits near water, heat, or an exterior wall.\n\nBangladesh Meteorological Department publishes monthly humidity normals because humidity changes by season and location. In a home, leaks, wet cleaning, steam, poor ventilation, and condensation can create far more severe local exposure than the outdoor average. “Moisture resistant” should never be read as “waterproof.”\n\nSeparate normal dry-area use from splash risk, persistent dampness, and direct water contact. Fix the moisture source first. A decorative sheet cannot compensate for leaking plumbing or a wet substrate.\n\nThese board names describe different constructions, not one quality ladder. The USDA Forest Products Laboratory explains that panel properties change with wood elements, adhesives, construction, and processing. The board name alone does not predict performance.\n\nPlywood is built from bonded veneer layers. It is available in different grades and bond classifications, with meaningful differences in face quality, internal defects, adhesive performance, and intended exposure. Bangladesh Standard BDS 799:2006 covers different grades and types of plywood for general purposes and defines defects such as blisters, borer holes, and delamination.','images/dit-studio-logo.webp','6 min read','2026-08-04 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22','2026-10-13 00:00:22');
